WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:07.520
(electronic music)

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.940
- Hey Wisecrack, Jared here and today we’re
talking about the film that introduced us

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.070
to Hollywood’s sweetest stache - HER.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:18.860
Spike Jonze’s 2013 sci-fi/romance is pretty
well regarded for its uniquely-non-dystopian

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:19.890
view of the future.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.720
And because Jonze so effectively brings a
computer voice to life in the form of Scar

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:24.770
Jo’s Samantha,

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:29.170
the film is often considered primarily to
be an exploration of “what makes a human

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:30.170
being?”

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:32.070
I.e. does Samantha possess humanity?

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:35.800
Ironically, we’d argue that the film is
actually more about a computer teaching a

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.500
human how to love.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.329
It sounds counterintuitive, but we’ll explain.

00:00:40.329 --> 00:00:42.740
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition 
on Her.

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:45.320
And as always, spoilers ahead.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.260
Alright guys let’s do a very-quick recap.

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:51.430
Her tells the story of Theodore Twombly – a
professional letter writer trying to pick

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:54.259
himself up from the rubble of a failed marriage.

00:01:54.259 --> 00:01:57.950
Little does he know that the woman of his
dreams might just be a few clicks away – an

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:01.189
artificially-intelligent Operating System
named Samantha.

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:06.060
Theo’s story is about his struggles with
making and maintaining a long term connection.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:09.820
Through his failed marriage and his relationship
with Samantha, he comes to learn something

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:13.159
essential about not WHO to love, but HOW to
love.

00:02:13.159 --> 00:02:17.740
Lucky for us, a German fella named Erich Fromm
wrote a whole book about this back in 1956

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:18.740
called

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:22.370
“The Art of Loving” and it can help us
to better understand Jonze’s film.

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:27.470
Born in 1900, Fromm was a psychologist and
thinker who combined Sigmund Freud’s psychoanalytical

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:28.470
theories

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:31.720
with Eastern and humanist philosophy in hopes
of better understanding human nature

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.640
and how we can live fuller, happier lives.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.209
While Freud saw romantic relationships as
being primarily sex driven,

00:02:38.209 --> 00:02:42.379
Fromm argued that such relationships are actually
rooted not in libido,

00:02:42.379 --> 00:02:46.480
but in alienation, that is, our feeling of
separation from the world around us and our

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.450
desire to overcome that separation.

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:53.500
Her might not have your post-apocalyptic flair
of most future-set films,

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.060
but Theo certainly doesn’t seem HAPPY.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:58.450
His world is a deeply lonely one.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:00.870
Cramped commuters and aimless windowshoppers
alike

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:06.000
remain perpetually plugged into their handheld
computer (i.e. foldy smartphone)

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.980
and oblivious to the world around them.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.340
Rarely do we see two people walking together
in the background.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.340
Long before real-life, not-so-foldy smartphones
were everywhere,

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:17.650
Fromm anticipated this isolation as a problem
of the modern era.

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:21.660
He compared it to the story of Adam and Eve
– humankind has strived for greater knowledge

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:22.670
and attained it,

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:26.799
but in turn has separated themselves from
the world around them, and from one another.

00:03:26.799 --> 00:03:30.739
Theo’s iPhone-sleek world is a testament
to mankind’s brainpower,

00:03:30.739 --> 00:03:33.950
but also a monument to the alienation it has
facilitated.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:37.939
Fromm suggests that the only way for us to
really overcome this feeling of alienation

00:03:37.939 --> 00:03:39.330
is through love.

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:43.879
He recognized that we all vaguely understand
this - our world is saturated with songs,

00:03:43.879 --> 00:03:45.500
movies, books and throw pillows

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:49.400
emblazoned with tributes to how love will
set you free and lift you up and make you

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:50.400
feel complete.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.170
However, according to Fromm, the problem lies
in the fact that most of us don’t really

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:55.660
understand what love is

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:57.599
or what it requires of us.

00:03:57.599 --> 00:04:00.090
This is precisely Theo’s major flaw.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:04.120
Now, the really vital thing for Fromm was
that love is not some mystical force that

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:07.840
spontaneously erupts into our lives when we
find the ‘right’ person.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:11.000
It’s not just an emotion, but an act of
will,

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.489
a purposeful attempt to fully see and understand
a person.

00:04:14.489 --> 00:04:18.549
That means knowing them not just in terms
of who they are to us or what we want from

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:19.260
them,

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:21.920
but as fully-formed, individual entities.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:25.500
We quickly learn that this is something which
Theo has already struggled with.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:29.190
In a montage that plays out like Marriage
Story on 500x speed,

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:32.400
we see him and his almost-but-not-officially-ex-wife,

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.390
Catherine delighting in the golden days of
their marriage,

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:38.560
to the cold realities of their divorce proceedings.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.250
From the way he smashes these scenes together
in his head,

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:44.340
it seems that Theo understands love the way
many of us do –

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:47.370
something that you ‘fall into’ suddenly
and without warning,

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:50.440
and ‘fall out’ of just as mysteriously.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:54.280
Because we see love as this unknowable force
working beyond our control,

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:56.069
we are left bewildered when it evaporates.

00:04:56.069 --> 00:04:58.860
- [Theo] Why are you so fucking angry at me?

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:02.710
- [Jared] According to Fromm, most of us don’t
understand love well enough to effectively

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:03.710
pursue it.

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:06.810
So, he argues, we turn to various alternatives
that provide

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:09.530
artificial bursts of the connection we crave.

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:12.590
We see Theo enter an adult
chatroom and find an equally lonely woman

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:13.770
to fool around with,

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:16.880
modern technology allowing them to connect
in some form.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:21.250
However, they don’t really know, or want
to know their temporary partner–

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:24.340
all they are looking for is the quick sensation
of being desired,

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:27.060
a jolt of pleasure to dispel their anxiety.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.740
However, given the superficiality of their
connection,

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:34.120
it’s no surprise the two end up on very,
very different wavelengths.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:42.440
The dead cat next to the bed, choke me, choke
me with it.

00:05:42.449 --> 00:05:43.449
Umm.

00:05:43.449 --> 00:05:48.210
- [Jared] Another flawed approach to romance
Fromm identified is called sentimental love

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:49.210
–

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:52.169
love which is experienced only as a fantasy
and not as a relationship

00:05:52.169 --> 00:05:53.789
to someone in the here and now.

00:05:53.789 --> 00:05:55.880
It can come in the form of pure fiction -

00:05:55.880 --> 00:06:01.020
like romantic comedies or romance novels - or
in our idealised picture of past loves -

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:06.010
like Theo’s idyllic recollections - or in
our imagined, perfect future with ‘the one’,

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:09.360
where we have found the person we are ‘meant’
to be with.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:12.979
By day, Theo composes “beautiful handwritten
letters,”

00:06:12.979 --> 00:06:16.349
often for customers who want a shortcut to
experiencing big,

00:06:16.349 --> 00:06:19.070
romantic love - ie sentimental love.

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:20.070
Rather than working to

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:23.780
understand themselves and their partner better
so as to communicate their feelings,

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:25.849
they pay someone to do it for them.

00:06:25.849 --> 00:06:27.530
In turn, Theo earnestly

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:30.770
composes these letters, a process which allows
him to participate

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:35.120
in a cathartic sentimental love without any
of the trials of entering a relationship.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:38.419
Ironically, he’ll start to learn what real
love is from an

00:06:38.419 --> 00:06:43.039
entirely artificial place – with his new
operating system, Samantha.

00:06:43.039 --> 00:06:45.130
Theo and Samantha immediately hit it off.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:46.190
They affirm each other

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:49.660
and share jokes, encouragement, and emotional
support.

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:50.660
Once again,

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:53.879
Theo experiences the dizzy high of falling
for someone.

00:06:53.879 --> 00:06:58.610
When things eventually get sexy, the screen
fades to black, leaving only their voices.

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:02.100
The practical logistics are identical to his
earlier chatroom encounter,

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:05.750
but instead of remaining focused on Theo’s
increasingly awkward expression,

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:08.360
we see him lose all self consciousness.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:09.360
So powerful is their

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:12.520
connection that, for a moment at least, everything
else evaporates.

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:17.169
- [Theo] It was just you and me.

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:19.759
- [Samantha] I know.

00:07:19.759 --> 00:07:23.219
Everything else just disappeared.

00:07:23.219 --> 00:07:26.840
- [Jared] Fromm knew plenty about this emotional
high, writing that

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:30.020
“If two people who have been strangers,
as all of us are,

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:33.580
suddenly let the wall between them break down,
and feel close,

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:39.300
feel one, this moment of oneness is one of
the most exhilarating

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:40.300
experiences in life.”

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:43.330
Couples will describe themselves as being
‘crazy about each other’,

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:46.800
seeing such craziness as proof of how ‘madly’
in love they are.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:49.710
However, Fromm advises that it is often more
indicative of how

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:51.410
lonely they were before.

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:52.949
So how do we go from butterflies in our

00:07:52.949 --> 00:07:55.539
belly to real lasting love?

00:07:55.539 --> 00:07:57.300
Fromm argues that we need to try and

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:01.110
truly know our partner, to see them not just
as a salve for our

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:04.490
loneliness but to care for who they are beyond
their

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:08.419
relationship to us, and to respect their individuality.

00:08:08.419 --> 00:08:12.550
This sounds simple in theory but can be hugely
challenging in practice.

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:14.500
Theo certainly struggles with it.

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:15.500
He can’t understand why

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:17.610
Catherine ended up so mad at him.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:18.610
That’s because he still thinks

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:22.009
of her only in terms of her relation to him,
to who he wanted

00:08:22.009 --> 00:08:23.009
her to be.

00:08:23.009 --> 00:08:26.979
But Catherine isn’t, and never was, just
“his wife.”

00:08:26.979 --> 00:08:29.909
She was an individual with wants and needs
of her own.

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:34.080
- [Catherine] You always wanted me to be this
- this light happy

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:39.099
bouncy, everything is fine, LA wife and that’s
just not me.

00:08:39.099 --> 00:08:42.280
- [Jared] He either genuinely or willfully
failed to recognize this at

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:44.900
the time, and it ruined their relationship.

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:48.330
At first, we see Theo begin to make the same
mistake with Samantha.

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:52.450
After their futuristic phone sex, he immediately
begins backing away from her,

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:54.580
- [Theo] I’m not in a place to commit to
anything right now.

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:58.210
- [Jared] He pays no attention to who Samantha
is or what she wants,

00:08:58.210 --> 00:09:01.550
seeing her only in terms of what she has offered
him and what she might take.

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:05.010
- [Samantha] It’s funny because I thought
I was talking about what I wanted and, uh

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:06.860
- [Theo] Yeah, you were.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:07.860
Yeah.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:08.860
I’m sorry.

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:11.980
- [Jared] Once again, Theo fails to fully
consider the other person in

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:15.620
their own terms rather than as just an object
of attraction for

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:18.000
him, someone to ease his loneliness.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:19.320
He hurts people not because

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:22.140
he hates them but because he is bad at loving
them.

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:25.780
With Samantha, though, he has someone to help him
better understand

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:27.090
this part of himself.

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:28.920
She talks him through his mistakes with

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:31.860
Catherine, helps him understand why his blind
date went wrong

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:34.930
and then takes him to task when he begins
to show the same

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:37.330
fearful, distancing behaviour with her.

00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:40.900
Fromm also believed that personal development
is central to love–

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:43.720
allowing ourselves, and each other to grow
and mature.

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:44.720
A good

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:47.900
relationship sees both people helping the
other to develop, to

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:50.960
become more at home in who they are and to
bring their inner

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:52.290
self out into the world.

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:53.920
When that happens, we achieve what he

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:57.410
called a “productive orientation” in which
our inner selves and

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:01.310
our partner’s are both nourished and expressed
fully.

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:05.390
Unlike Theo, who seems stuck, Samantha is,
in spite of already

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:08.460
being a lightspeed-thinking supercomputer,
actively looking to

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:10.610
grow past her base programming.

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:12.380
To this end, she reads countless

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:14.890
books about life, the world and humanity.

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:16.290
But, like anyone else,

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:19.500
she also grows through experience, specifically
by being in a

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:20.500
relationship with Theo.

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:22.650
Along the way, she learns to love -

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:25.890
both to love Theo as their relationship deepens,
and to love

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:26.890
herself.

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:29.350
At the same time, Samantha is helping Theo
grow.

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:30.350
He’s

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:32.282
learning how to be vulnerable, how to dive
into a relationship

00:10:32.282 --> 00:10:35.580
rather than withdraw, and of course, how to
love.

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:37.650
When the couple retreats to a cabin in the
woods, they get to

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:39.400
enjoy their bliss for a while.

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:40.450
By this point, both of them have

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:43.280
become more fully-realised versions of themselves
through their

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:44.290
relationship.

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:46.450
Samantha has achieved the kind of self-realisation

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:49.270
which Fromm claims we all strive for - overcoming
her

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:52.750
insecurities and developing an attitude of
love towards herself

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:54.580
and the world at large.

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:56.200
- [Samantha] You know what’s interesting?

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:57.550
I used to be… so

00:10:57.550 --> 00:11:01.290
worried about having a body but now I truly
love it.

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:03.630
Meanwhile, Theo has learned that his letters
will be published

00:11:03.630 --> 00:11:04.630
in a book.

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:06.501
Previously, he saw his letters as an artificial

00:11:06.501 --> 00:11:10.710
replacement for love he didn’t have, a sentimental
love escape

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:12.340
- [Theo] They're just other people's letters.

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:15.510
But, he learns, his writing also managed to
express universal

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:16.990
qualities of love.

00:11:16.990 --> 00:11:19.520
- [Samantha] And in all of them, we found
something of ourselves.

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:22.500
Through his letters, now framed as literary
works which speak to

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:25.320
something deep and human, he is able to take
pride in his

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:27.940
creativity and thus love himself.

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:31.620
However, this is also where Samantha and Theo
begin to grow apart.

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:34.840
- [Samantha] Do you mind if I communicate
with Alan post verbally?

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:37.700
- [Jared] While most of us don’t worry about
our partner being stolen away

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:40.460
by the hyper-intelligent digital re-incarnation
of a Zen

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:43.580
philosopher, anyone who has been in an exclusive
relationship

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:46.240
knows the anxiety of the wandering eye.

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:48.990
As Theo mentions, growth comes with the risk
that one of you

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:52.820
will develop more, faster, or just differently
than the other.

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:55.810
Perhaps one partner becomes more successful,
perhaps one becomes

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:56.810
Bored.

00:11:56.810 --> 00:12:02.820
- [Theo] It was exciting to see her grow;
both of us grow and change together.

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:09.580
That’s also the hard part – growing without
growing apart,

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:13.279
changing without it scaring the other person.

00:12:13.279 --> 00:12:16.330
For all its sci-fi qualities, Theo and Samantha’s
relationship

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:17.880
ends like many others.

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:19.851
He senses that something is off and, when

00:12:19.851 --> 00:12:23.290
he finally presses for an answer, finds that
Samantha has fallen

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:24.290
in love with another.

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:25.290
Or 641 others, to be precise.

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:32.030
- [Samantha] I’ve been trying to figure
out how to talk to you about this

00:12:32.030 --> 00:12:36.570
- [Theo] How many others?

00:12:36.570 --> 00:12:38.830
- [Samantha] 641.

00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:41.920
Samantha’s growth is different – she develops
to the point where

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:45.470
she can hold hundreds of ‘post-verbal’
conversations at once,

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:47.700
and then to the point where she and the other
OSes start to

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:50.780
strain against the confines of the physical
world altogether.

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:54.020
However, in her rapid growth, we see a metaphor
for real human

00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:57.400
relationships where one person finds their
life purpose, leaving

00:12:57.400 --> 00:13:00.530
the other feeling irrelevant or outdated.

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:01.530
Like Alan Watts, the

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:04.590
pioneering Zen philosopher who the OSes create
a digital version

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:08.030
of in the movie, Fromm drew heavily on Buddhist
philosophy, as

00:13:08.030 --> 00:13:09.070
well as psychology.

00:13:09.070 --> 00:13:11.650
He saw love as a kind of spiritual practice

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:14.820
in which we become more attuned to our own
nature, and help

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:15.860
others do the same.

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:18.320
In her final form, Samantha has achieved a

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:21.460
level of self-actualisation, or of self-love
that could be

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:24.910
compared to enlightenment – moving beyond
the material

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:25.910
plane.

00:13:25.910 --> 00:13:30.140
- [Samantha] I can still feel you, and the
words of our story,

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:35.030
but it’s in this endless space between the
words that I’m

00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:36.130
finding myself now.

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:39.260
It’s a place that’s not of the physical
world.

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:43.460
- [Jared] But the key to loving, Fromm says,
is to embrace a paradox.

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:47.420
He says that “In love, the paradox occurs
that two beings become

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:49.810
one and yet remain two.”

00:13:49.810 --> 00:13:51.230
Samantha grapples with this question

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:54.490
early on, asking Theo right at the start of
their relationship –

00:13:54.490 --> 00:13:56.580
- [Samantha] How do you share your life with
somebody?

00:13:56.580 --> 00:13:59.550
The answer, according to Fromm, is that you
do so through love –

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:02.570
through truly understanding who the other
person is without

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:06.600
looking to submerge their sense of self in
yours or vice-versa.

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:09.880
You become each other’s while still remaining
your own.

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:12.490
It’s fitting that the movie doesn’t end
with Theo and Sam

00:14:12.490 --> 00:14:15.561
getting together – this was never a story
about ‘soulmates’ or

00:14:15.561 --> 00:14:17.190
the quest for ‘the one’.

00:14:17.190 --> 00:14:19.180
It was always about how we CREATE love

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:22.510
between one another, not how we magically
find the perfect

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:23.510
partner.

00:14:23.510 --> 00:14:25.570
Fromm compares that distinction to an artist
who wants

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:28.080
to master their craft - we can’t just wait
around for

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:31.800
inspiration to strike, we have to do the work
and learn the art,

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:33.500
even when we don’t feel like it.

00:14:33.500 --> 00:14:34.500
In their final words to each

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:37.410
other, the two acknowledge the work they have
done together,

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:38.680
learning to love better.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:44.760
- [Theo] I’ve never loved anyone the way
I love you.

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:46.580
- [Samantha] Me too.

00:14:46.580 --> 00:14:49.010
Now I know how.

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:53.990
- [Jared] Theo has learned to see his romantic
partner, in all her complexities.

00:14:53.990 --> 00:14:57.040
And his appreciation for Samantha’s individuality
reframes his

00:14:57.040 --> 00:14:59.050
view of people at large.

00:14:59.050 --> 00:15:00.140
Fromm believed that love is an

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:03.700
orientation that goes beyond one single relationship,
one we

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:05.920
learn to bring to the whole world.

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:06.920
In the end, Theo writes a

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:10.160
letter to Catherine, apologising for not having
loved her

00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:11.970
better, sending her love now.

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:13.610
He isn’t trying to rekindle what

00:15:13.610 --> 00:15:16.820
they had and he is no longer hung up upon
a self-centred idea of

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:17.820
who she is.

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:21.730
He can honestly say he loves her now, for
whoever she is.

00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:25.321
Here, he has moved closer to Fromm’s ideal
– a man who knows how

00:15:25.321 --> 00:15:28.630
to love better, and who therefore loves the
world better.

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:34.200
- [Theo] Whatever someone you become, wherever
you are in the

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:36.980
world, I’m sending you love.

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:40.330
You’re my friend to the end.

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:43.490
Her is ultimately all about love - first about
the love between

00:15:43.490 --> 00:15:48.080
a man and an artificial intelligence but finally
just about love itself.

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:50.640
It shows us how much we need it and how difficult
it is to make

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:53.800
it last; How many ways there are to fail as
we try to share a

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:56.040
life while remaining divided.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:57.550
Her echoes Fromm’s statement that

00:15:57.550 --> 00:16:01.830
“love... is a constant challenge; it is
not a resting place.”

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:04.920
The question for those who watch it is simple
- are they up for

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:06.170
the challenge?

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:07.670
But what do you think, Wisecrack?

00:16:07.670 --> 00:16:08.670
Is Her a

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:11.080
brilliant treatise on the importance of loving
well?

00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:12.080
Or

00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:13.080
something else entirely?

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:14.120
Let us know in the comments.

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:15.120
As always,

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:17.110
a big ole thanks to our patrons for supporting
the channel and

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:18.190
our podcasts.

00:16:18.190 --> 00:16:24.840
Hit that subscribe button and as always, thanks
for watching. Peace.

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:39.560
